    {
      "field_name": "diagnostic_criteria",
      "value": "clinical features such as rigidity, muscle spasms, brainstem signs and hyperekplexia; EMG showing continuous motor-unit activity; anti-glycine receptor antibodies identified in serum and CSF; response to immunosuppression",
      "quote": "Progressive encephalomyelitis with rigidity and myoclonus (PERM) is a rare disorder presenting subacutely with rigidity, muscle spasms, brainstem signs and hyperekplexia. Investigations revealed CSF lymphocytic pleocytosis and simultaneous concentric needle EMG was consistent with a diagnosis of PERM. Anti-glycine receptor antibodies were identified when patient sera were tested for binding to human embryonic kidney cells transfected with EGFP-tagged glycine receptor α1 subunit. The patient's serum bound strongly to the GlyR-EGFP expressing cells. A diagnosis of anti-glycine receptor mediated PERM was made. The patient responded to initial intense immunosuppression and remained stable for many years with no further immunomodulation.",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
